{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#470","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#470","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#470\",\"parent_revision\":\"1536f59f9ecbb37d6c65c66c5e76d41bda25c2f6\",\"root_revision\":\"4c4f903006ccf56df589d680d729e2352b5099e2622ff5a01f403557d344a881\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_close` and `backlog_resolve` permanently close GitHub issues and optionally delete local files. These operations are irreversible. Currently they execute immediately upon invocation \u2014 there is no confirmation step.\\n\\nFastMCP v3 provides `ctx.elicit()` for server-side mid-execution user prompts. This allows a tool to pause and ask \\\"Are you sure?\\\" before committing a destructive action. The client renders the confirmation UI; the tool resumes only on `accept`.\\n\\nFiles affected: `.claude/skills/backlog/backlog_core/server.py`\\n\\nWork required:\\n- Add `ctx: Context` to backlog_close and backlog_resolve\\n- Before executing the irreversible GitHub close call, `await ctx.elicit(\\\"Close #{issue}: {title}?\\\", response_type=None)`\\n- On `decline` or `cancel`, return early with a safe message\\n- Gate this behind a parameter `confirm: bool = True` so automated callers (CI, scripts) can skip elicitation\\n\\nNote: elicitation requires the client to implement an elicitation handler. Tools must handle the case where the client does not support it.\\n\\nSource: FastMCP v3 docs `servers/elicitation.mdx` \u2014 `ctx.elicit(message, response_type=None)` is approval-only elicitation.\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAssessment date: 2026-04-23\\n\\n| # | Condition | Status | Source |\\n|---|-----------|--------|--------|\\n| 1 | FastMCP v3 `ctx.elicit()` API verified | AVAILABLE | Item description cites `servers/elicitation.mdx` |\\n| 2 | `backlog_close` and `backlog_resolve` in server.py | AVAILABLE | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` |\\n| 3 | Claude Code client supports elicitation handler | DERIVABLE | Check Claude Code MCP client capabilities |\\n| 4 | FastMCP version supports ctx.elicit() | DERIVABLE | Check pyproject.toml fastmcp version |\\n\\nVerdict: READY \u2014 all information available or derivable.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **add elicitation to backlog_close and backlog_resolve for confirmation of destructive operations** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: fastmcp-creator skill review of backlog_core/server.py\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-03-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-04-23)\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\nP2 Feature. Low complexity. Additive safety guard on two tools. Low risk.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n- `backlog_close` and `backlog_resolve` accept `ctx: Context` as a FastMCP dependency\\n- Before executing the irreversible GitHub close call, `await ctx.elicit('Close #{issue}: {title}?', response_type=None)` is called\\n- On `decline` or `cancel`, tool returns early with message 'Cancelled \u2014 issue not closed'\\n- `confirm: bool = True` parameter added: when `False`, elicitation is skipped (for automated callers like CI)\\n- Tool handles the case where the client does not support elicitation (fallback: proceed with warning)\\n- Regression: calling with `confirm=False` produces identical behavior to current implementation\\n\\n### Files\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 add ctx.elicit() to backlog_close and backlog_resolve\\n\\n### Story\\n\\nAs a caller of `backlog_close` or `backlog_resolve`, I need a confirmation prompt before these irreversible operations execute so I can catch accidental invocations without reverting closed issues manually.\\n\",\"digest\":\"23711607e4b9409ec9ae3faeab14230507bf58e714c4e83076d682507c883256\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPg0mWg\"}"}